---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Initial from '../components/primitives/Initial.astro'
import CodeWindow from '../components/primitives/CodeWindow.astro'
import WayOfThinkingSketch from '../components/blocks/WayOfThinkingSketch.astro'
import jacquard from '@fontsource/jacquard-12/files/jacquard-12-latin-400-normal.woff2?url'
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Why state lives outside UI"
  description="Why the authors of Nano Stores keep state, computations and lifecycle out of components, and what that costs."
  preload={[jacquard]}
>
  <article class="leaf essay">
    <p class="incipit mono">How we think</p>
    <h1>Why state lives outside UI</h1>
    <div class="column">
      <p class="lead">
        <Initial>W</Initial>hen logic leaves the components, a framework becomes
        a simple rendering layer. We think much of the complexity we attribute
        to frameworks comes from the logic we write inside them.
      </p>
      <WayOfThinkingSketch />
      <p class="idea">
        This is the idea Nano Stores is built around, and it is our opinion, not
        an API rule: we think a component should draw state, not own it.
      </p>

      <section>
        <h2>The problem</h2>
        <p>
          It is easy to write a feature inside a component, and we understand
          why. Loading, derived values and cleanup all fit next to the markup:
        </p>
        <CodeWindow
          file="logic-outside-hooks/before.tsx"
          caption="Header.tsx"
        />
        <p>
          It works, but we don't like where the model of the feature ends up: in
          the render cycle. Where the data comes from, when it loads and what
          depends on what are written as effects and dependency arrays, in the
          terms of one framework.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What we think it costs</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Testing.</strong> To check that the admin flag is derived
            correctly, the test has to render the component in a framework's
            test environment and emulate its lifecycle.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Reuse.</strong> The next screen that needs{' '}
            <code>isAdmin</code> either copies the effect or lifts the state
            into a provider. In both cases the component tree comes along.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Moving.</strong> Another framework, or a second client such
            as React Native, means rewriting the logic, because it is written in
            the framework's terms.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The other way</h2>
        <p>
          We'd rather have the store own the state, the computations and the
          lifecycle. The component then renders the ready value, and the link to
          the framework is one line at the boundary:
        </p>
        <CodeWindow file="why/user.ts" caption="stores/user.ts" store />
        <CodeWindow file="why/Header.tsx" caption="Header.tsx" />
        <p>
          <code>onMount</code> starts loading when something subscribes,{' '}
          <code>task</code> marks the loading so that tests and server rendering
          can wait for it, and <code>computed</code> derives the flag (see{' '}
          <a
            href="/docs/concepts/smart-stores/"
            data-track="cta_click"
            data-placement="why-reference"
          >
            Smart stores
          </a>
          ). <code>useStore</code> is the only React-specific line. Vue, Svelte
          and the other{' '}
          <a
            href="/docs/frameworks/react-preact/"
            data-track="cta_click"
            data-placement="why-reference"
          >
            frameworks
          </a>{' '}
          have their own one-line equivalent.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Testing a store</h2>
        <p>
          To test the admin flag in the component version, the test renders the
          component in a DOM environment and waits for the rendered output:
        </p>
        <CodeWindow file="why/before.test.tsx.txt" caption="Header.test.tsx" />
        <p>
          The store is plain JavaScript, so its test needs no renderer and no
          DOM, which is a large part of why we prefer this shape.{' '}
          <code>keepMount</code> keeps the store active during the test,{' '}
          <code>allTasks</code> waits for the loading started by{' '}
          <code>task</code>, and <code>cleanStores</code> resets the store
          afterwards (see{' '}
          <a
            href="/docs/guides/tests/"
            data-track="cta_click"
            data-placement="why-reference"
          >
            Tests
          </a>
          ):
        </p>
        <CodeWindow file="why/user.test.ts" caption="stores/user.test.ts" />
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>UI as a skin</h2>
        <p>
          We like to think of a component as a skin over the state. It decides
          how things look, and the store decides what is true. Swap the skin,
          whether it is another framework, another platform or a test, and the
          state and its logic stay the same.
        </p>
        <p>
          The shape is a familiar one. If it sounds like MVC, it is: stores are
          the model, components are the view.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What it costs us</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            The logic is not next to the markup. Reading a feature means opening
            the store and the component.
          </li>
          <li>
            State that belongs to one component and dies with it doesn't need a
            store, and we'd keep a local <code>useState</code> there.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <div class="end">
        <p class="explicit" aria-hidden="true">
          Explicit
        </p>
        <a
          class="back"
          href="/"
          data-track="cta_click"
          data-placement="why-next"
        >
          <span class="manicule manicule-back" aria-hidden="true"></span> Find
          your situation
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  /*
   * An essay on the manuscript leaf (layout.css): the leaf's margins, its
   * text column as the reading column, the home page's reading scale.
   */
  article {
    padding: 64px var(--leaf-pad) 0;
  }
  /* The incipit: the opening words a scribe wrote in red. Color in roles.css. */
  .incipit {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    font:
      400 12px/20px 'Fragment Mono',
      monospace;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(38px, 6vw, 60px);
    font-stretch: 78%;
    line-height: 1.09;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
  }
  .column .lead {
    margin-block: 24px 32px;
    font-size: 21px;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
  /*
   * The lead opens with a decorative initial (Initial.astro) dropped two
   * lines. Jacquard's capitals are 0.57em tall, Instrument Sans' 0.72em; at
   * line-height 1.55 the lead's first cap line sits 0.415em below its top
   * and the second baseline 2.27em below that cap line. So the initial is
   * 2.27 / 0.57 = 3.98em of the lead, its box exactly one capital tall,
   * nudged down to the first cap line. The box ends inside the second line,
   * so two lines wrap around it.
   */
  .column .lead :global(.initial) {
    width: auto;
    margin: 0.054em 0.1em 0 -0.02em;
    font-size: 3.98em;
    line-height: 0.57;
    transform: none;
  }
  .column p,
  li {
    font-size: 18px;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  section {
    margin-top: 56px;
  }
  /*
   * Section headings at the home section scale, in ink. A rubric pilcrow
   * (the text face's own ¶) hangs in the margin so the column keeps its
   * edge; on phones it stands inline. Hidden from screen readers.
   */
  h2 {
    position: relative;
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    font-size: 32px;
    line-height: 1.125;
  }
  h2::before {
    content: '¶' / '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    right: 100%;
    margin-right: 0.3em;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-stretch: 100%;
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }
  ul {
    padding-left: 1.2em;
  }
  section :global(.code-window) {
    margin-block: 16px;
  }
  /* Links in the text: ink, a faded iron-gall underline (roles.css). */
  section a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration-line: underline;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-underline-offset: 0.22em;
    text-decoration-skip-ink: auto;
  }
  section a:hover {
    text-decoration-thickness: 1.5px;
  }
  /*
   * The end: a single rule across the leaf, then the
   * scribe's closing word and the way back, centered. The closing word
   * is decoration only.
   */
  .end {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    margin: 64px calc(-1 * var(--leaf-pad)) 0;
    padding: 56px var(--leaf-pad) 64px;
    text-align: center;
    box-shadow: inset 0 1px var(--line);
  }
  .end p {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .end .explicit {
    margin-bottom: 12px;
    font:
      400 11px/20px 'Fragment Mono',
      monospace;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .back {
    font-size: 18px;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.55;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  /* A hand points back home, as on the situation pages. */
  .back .manicule {
    margin-right: 0.3em;
    vertical-align: -0.1em;
  }
  @media (width < 37.5rem) {
    h1 {
      line-height: 1.12;
    }
    .column .lead {
      font-size: 18px;
    }
    section {
      margin-top: 44px;
    }
    h2 {
      font-size: 28px;
      line-height: 1.15;
    }
    h2::before {
      position: static;
      margin-right: 0.25em;
    }
  }
</style>
